= Energy conversion =

== Overview ==
'''Energy conversion''' is the transformation of energy from one physical form or carrier into another. Conversion can occur in natural processes, machines, electrical networks and living systems. It does not create or destroy total physical energy in an isolated system.

== Examples ==
A photovoltaic device converts some incident radiant energy into electrical energy. A generator converts mechanical energy into electrical energy. A combustion engine converts chemical energy through thermal processes into some mechanical work and other energy transfers. A biological organism can convert chemical energy from food into mechanical activity and heat.

Each example requires a specified input, output, operating condition and physical system.

== Conservation and efficiency ==
[[Related to::Energy|Energy]] is conserved in a suitably complete physical accounting, while useful outputs may represent only a portion of inputs. [[Related to::Energy efficiency|Energy efficiency]] quantifies useful output relative to input for a defined service or process. It cannot be calculated meaningfully without the right unit conventions and a specified system boundary.

== Energy carriers, sources, and storage ==
Electricity is often treated as an energy carrier: its supply may be generated using different primary energy sources, then used by motors, electronics or heaters. A storage device temporarily converts and retains energy in an accessible physical form and later delivers some portion back. Round-trip efficiency differs from instantaneous conversion efficiency; timing, degradation, losses and auxiliary loads affect actual performance.

== Measurement and lifecycle boundaries ==
A local converter may be efficient while upstream fuel extraction, generation, transmission or cooling introduces additional losses. The accounting boundary and definition of useful output determine which comparison is meaningful. A higher device efficiency does not automatically establish lower system-wide total energy use, expense or climate impacts.

== Sources and limitations ==
Conversion processes depend on thermodynamic, electrical, mechanical or chemical laws. “100% efficiency” can have different meanings depending on whether useful output counts electricity, work, heat or total recovered energy. An engineering comparison must state what is counted.

